      model: 'Because the bank is listed with the SEC, it must comply with the Sarbanes-Oxley Act. It must report its financial results quarterly under Section 302, and each year its management must confirm under Section 404 that sufficient internal controls guarantee the correctness of these results. The transaction bank is not listed itself, but it is indirectly affected, because its securities processing influences the financial results of its client. The listed bank must therefore confirm that the contribution of all outsourced processes is correct, and this must be verified by neutral external auditors. To prove that its controls work, the service provider can present a SAS 70 report. A Type I report only confirms that the controls are documented and exist, whereas a Type II report also confirms that the controls actually functioned over at least six months. For SOx purposes, a Type II report is much stronger evidence. In conclusion, the bank can outsource the work but not its responsibility for correct financial reporting.',
